Let $f(x)=-\frac{(x-3)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{-x^{2}+6x-9}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=-x^{\frac{3}{2}}+6x^{\frac{1}{2}}-9x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=-\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+3x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{9}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(4,-\frac{1}{2}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(4)&=-\frac{3}{2}\left(4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+3\left(4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{9}{2}\left(4\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=-3+\frac{3}{2}+\frac{9}{16}\\
&=-\frac{15}{16}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(-\frac{15}{16}\right)}\\
&=\frac{16}{15}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $f(x)=-\frac{(x+1)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{-x^{2}-2x-1}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=-x^{\frac{3}{2}}-2x^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=-\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{1}{2}x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(9,-\frac{100}{3}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(9)&=-\frac{3}{2}\left(9\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-\left(9\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}+\frac{1}{2}\left(9\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=-\frac{9}{2}-\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{54}\\
&=-\frac{130}{27}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(-\frac{130}{27}\right)}\\
&=\frac{27}{130}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $f(x)=\frac{(x+2)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}+4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}+4x^{\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(1,9\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(1)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(1\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+2\left(1\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(1\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=\frac{3}{2}+2-2\\
&=\frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

